 "introductionText": "What FRUS answers directly: the series prints the Department's own statements of the test it applied to a Latin American government that had taken power by force, from Evarts's wait before recognizing Díaz in 1878 to the 1979 recognition of Nicaragua's provisional government, and in each case what it asked of the new government first: control of the country, the people's acquiescence, a promise to honour international obligations, and, in some periods, constitutional origin or a dated return to elections. Where FRUS points for the rest: the printed documents came almost entirely from the Department of State's central decimal file (Record Group 59), chiefly the 'political affairs' and 'government' classes for each country; the editors' footnotes cite the same file, the Bureau of Inter-American Affairs lot files, and the presidential libraries, and after 1963 the Central Foreign Policy File's POL 16 (Independence. Recognition.) category. What this request cannot get from FRUS: the texts of the inter-American resolutions on recognition and of the Estrada and Tobar doctrines, the notes of recognition after 1963, and any case before 1876 under these headings; the series mentions them and points to the shelf but does not print them.",
