   "text": "Before Wilson: control, consent and obligations, 1877–1914",
   "level": 1
  },
  {
   "kind": "prose",
   "text": "The oldest cases in the series state the test the Department applied to a government that had taken power by arms: did it hold the country, did the people acquiesce, and would it meet the republic's international obligations. Nothing in these instructions asks how the government came to power. The documents run from the year-long wait before recognizing Porfirio Díaz to the two-line telegram recognizing Peru's junta in 1914."
  },
