   "kind": "heading",
   "text": "Obligations as the price, and the Central American treaty rule, 1921–1938",
   "level": 1
  },
  {
   "kind": "prose",
   "text": "In the 1920s the Department asked a government that had come to power by force to bind itself first: Hughes made a treaty protecting property rights the act of recognition itself in Mexico, and in Central America the 1923 General Treaty's Article II made non-recognition of coup governments a treaty obligation the United States enforced. Both conditions were given up in the 1930s; the Legal Adviser's 1938 opinion on Brazil shows the narrower rule that replaced them."
  },
  {
   "kind": "document",
   "volumeId": "frus1921v02",
   "documentId": "d344",
   "notes": [
    "Agent finding, Recognition study, 2026-10-25: Hughes tells Summerlin that recognition is subordinate: a treaty binding Mexico to protect persons and validly acquired property would itself accomplish recognition of the Obregón government."
   ]
  },
  {
   "kind": "excerpt",
   "volumeId": "frus1921v02",
   "documentId": "d344",
   "text": "The question of recognition is a subordinate one"
  },
  {
   "kind": "excerpt",
   "volumeId": "frus1921v02",
   "documentId": "d344",
   "text": "when it appears that there is a government in Mexico willing to bind itself to the discharge of primary international obligations, concurrently with that act its recognition will take place."
  },
